{:edb/db-before-t 1687 :edb/db-after-t 1688 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535000 50 #inst "2026-10-08T13:52:52.877Z" 13194139535000 true] [17592186216949 1100 "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}$.\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}$. According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$." 13194139535000 false] [17592186216949 1100 "The Sun has a mass of $(1.99\\times10^{30})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and Mars has a mass of $(6.42\\times10^{23})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\nThe average distance between the centers of the Sun and Mars is $(2.28\\times10^{11})\\,\\textrm{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" 13194139535000 true] [17592186216949 1145 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot ((1.99 \\cdot 10^{30}) \\cdot (6.42 \\cdot 10^{23}))/((2.28 \\cdot 10^{11})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (6.67 \\cdot 1.99 \\cdot 6.42)/((2.28)^{2}) \\cdot (10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{30} \\cdot 10^{23})/((10^{11})^{2}) \\\\\n&= (16.3925…) \\cdot 10^{20} \\\\\n&= (1.63925…) \\cdot 10^{21}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}$." 13194139535000 false] [17592186216949 1145 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of the Sun and $m_2$ be the mass of Mars. Then, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(1.99\\times {10}^{30})\\cdot (6.42\\times {10}^{23})}{(2.28\\times {10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 1.99\\cdot 6.42}{(2.28)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{30}\\cdot {10}^{23}}{({10}^{11})^{2}} \\\\  & =(16.392\\,5…)\\times {10}^{20} \\\\  & =(1.639\\,25…)\\times {10}^{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the gravitational force between the Sun and Mars is\n$\\Big(1.64 \\times 10^{21}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{N}.$" 13194139535000 true] [17592186216950 1100 "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}$. If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg}$, then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$." 13194139535000 false] [17592186216950 1100 "The centers of two objects are separated by a distance of $(3.0\\times10^3)\\,\\textrm{m}$ and the magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(3.0\\times10^{-4})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ If one object has mass $(2.0\\times10^5)\\,\\textrm{kg},$ then according to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the mass of the other object? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" 13194139535000 true] [17592186216950 1145 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{r^{2}} \\\\\nFr^{2} &= Gm_{1}m_{2} \\\\\nm_{2} &= \\frac{Fr^{2}}{Gm_{1}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm_{2} &= ((3.0 \\cdot 10^{-4}) \\cdot (3.0 \\cdot 10^{3})^{2})/((6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot (2.0 \\cdot 10^{5})) \\\\\n&= (3.0 \\cdot (3.0)^{2})/(6.67 \\cdot 2.0) \\cdot (10^{-4} \\cdot (10^{3})^{2})/(10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{5}) \\\\\n&= (2.023…) \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}$." 13194139535000 false] [17592186216950 1145 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nLet $m_2$ be the unknown mass.\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $m_2$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ F{r}^{2} & =G{m}_{1}{m}_{2} \\\\ {m}_{2} & =\\frac{F{r}^{2}}{G{m}_{1}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, substituting in the known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{2} & =\\frac{(3.0\\times {10}^{-4})\\cdot (3.0\\times {10}^{3})^{2}}{(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot (2.0\\times {10}^{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{3.0\\cdot (3.0)^{2}}{6.67\\cdot 2.0}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-4}\\cdot ({10}^{3})^{2}}{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{5}} \\\\  & =(2.023…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the mass of the other object is\n$\\Big(2.0 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{kg}.$" 13194139535000 true] [17592186216951 1100 "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}$. The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}$. According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$." 13194139535000 false] [17592186216951 1100 "Earth has a mass of $(5.97\\times10^{24})\\,\\textrm{kg}$ and the Moon has a mass of $(7.35\\times10^{22})\\,\\textrm{kg}.$ The magnitude of the gravitational force between them is $(1.98\\times10^{20})\\,\\textrm{N}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the distance between the centers of mass of Earth and the Moon? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **three significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2).$" 13194139535000 true] [17592186216951 1145 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N})$,\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg}$, and\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}$.\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{r^{2}} \\\\\nr^{2} &= G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{F} \\\\\nr &= \\sqrt{G\\frac{m_{1}m_{2}}{F}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr^{2} &= (6.67 \\cdot 10^{-11}) \\cdot ((5.97 \\cdot 10^{24}) \\cdot (7.35 \\cdot 10^{22}))/(1.98 \\cdot 10^{20}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6.67 \\cdot 5.97 \\cdot 7.35}{1.98} \\cdot \\frac{10^{-11} \\cdot 10^{24} \\cdot 10^{22}}{10^{20}} \\\\\n&= (147.816…) \\cdot 10^{15} \\\\\n&= (14.7816…) \\cdot 10^{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nr &= \\sqrt{14.7816… \\cdot 10^{16}} \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{14.7816…} \\cdot \\sqrt{10^{16}} \\\\\n&= (3.8446…) \\cdot 10^{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}$." 13194139535000 false] [17592186216951 1145 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF = G\\dfrac{m_1m_2}{r^2}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\textrm{N}),$\n\n- $G = (6.67\\times10^{-11})\\,\\textrm{m}^3/(\\textrm{kg}\\cdot\\textrm{s}^2)$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- $m_1$ and $m_2$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\textrm{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\textrm{m}.$\n\nFirst, let's rearrange the formula to make $r$ the subject:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{F}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $m_1$ be the mass of Earth and $m_2$ be the mass of the Moon. Then, substituting in the known values, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(5.97\\times {10}^{24})\\cdot (7.35\\times {10}^{22})}{1.98\\times {10}^{20}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 5.97\\cdot 7.35}{1.98}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{24}\\cdot {10}^{22}}{{10}^{20}} \\\\  & =(147.816…)\\times {10}^{15} \\\\  & =(14.7816…)\\times {10}^{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, taking the square root, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{14.7816…\\times {10}^{16}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{14.7816…}\\times \\sqrt{{10}^{16}} \\\\  & =(3.8446…)\\times {10}^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to three significant figures, the distance between Earth and the Moon is\n$\\Big(3.84 \\times 10^{8}\\Big) \\,\\textrm{m}.$" 13194139535000 true] [17592186217187 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325731 13194139535000 true] [17592186217187 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325736 13194139535000 true] [17592186217187 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325742 13194139535000 true] [17592186217187 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325747 13194139535000 true] [17592186217187 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325807 13194139535000 true] [17592186217188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325753 13194139535000 true] [17592186217188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325758 13194139535000 true] [17592186217188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325763 13194139535000 true] [17592186217188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325769 13194139535000 true] [17592186217188 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325775 13194139535000 true] [17592186217189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325780 13194139535000 true] [17592186217189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325785 13194139535000 true] [17592186217189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325791 13194139535000 true] [17592186217189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325796 13194139535000 true] [17592186217189 1020 #edb/ref 17592186325801 13194139535000 true] [17592186325731 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325732 13194139535000 true] [17592186325731 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325734 13194139535000 true] [17592186325731 1089 "q-336273" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325731 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535000 true] [17592186325731 1100 "The centers of two objects of masses $(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(9.0\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(2.5\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects? Write your answer in scientific notation, rounding to **two significant figures** where appropriate. Assume the Newtonian constant of gravitation is $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2}).$\n\nThe magnitude of the force between the two objects is\n$(${{field-1}}\n$\\times $\n{{field-2}}$)\\,\\text{N}.$" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325731 1113 #uuid "ec75030b-599a-5f41-bcd2-bdc4747e8a66" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325731 1145 "Newton's law of universal gravitation states that the gravitational force attracting two objects is proportional to the product of the two masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centers of mass:\n\n$$\nF=G\\frac{{m}_{1}{m}_{2}}{{r}^{2}}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n- $F$ is the gravitational force between the objects, measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}),$\n\n- $G=(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\,{\\text{m}}^{3}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot {\\text{s}}^{2})$ is the Newtonian constant of gravitation,\n\n- ${m}_{1}$ and ${m}_{2}$ are the masses of the objects, measured in $\\text{kg},$ and\n\n- $r$ is the distance between their centers of mass, measured in $\\text{m}.$\n\nChoosing ${m}_{1}=(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\,\\text{kg}$ and ${m}_{2}=(9.0\\times {10}^{7})\\,\\text{kg}$ and substituting in the other known values, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =(6.67\\times {10}^{-11})\\cdot \\frac{(3.4\\times {10}^{3})\\cdot (9.0\\times {10}^{7})}{(2.5\\times {10}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6.67\\cdot 3.4\\cdot 9.0}{(2.5)^{2}}\\times \\frac{{10}^{-11}\\cdot {10}^{3}\\cdot {10}^{7}}{({10}^{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =32.656\\,32\\times {10}^{-7} \\\\  & =3.265\\,632\\times {10}^{-6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, rounded to two significant figures, the magnitude of the gravitational force between the two objects is\n$(\\,3.3\\times {10}^{-6}\\,)\\,\\text{N}.$" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325732 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325733 13194139535000 true] [17592186325732 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535000 true] [17592186325732 1041 "field-1" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325732 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325733 13194139535000 true] [17592186325732 1110 #uuid "b90c36ac-309b-59e9-ba69-0f3aa10a52fa" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325733 1034 "3.3" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325733 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535000 true] [17592186325733 1111 #uuid "347894f1-b489-58d0-96fe-e4894b4878c2" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325734 1000 #edb/ref 17592186325735 13194139535000 true] [17592186325734 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535000 true] [17592186325734 1041 "field-2" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325734 1104 #edb/ref 17592186325735 13194139535000 true] [17592186325734 1110 #uuid "2a1e5040-179a-58fd-a1e0-ccaeab424db7" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325735 1034 "10^{-6}" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325735 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535000 true] [17592186325735 1111 #uuid "e5f8e132-5234-5b68-9dfc-d7db6f7141a6" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325736 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325737 13194139535000 true] [17592186325736 1017 #edb/ref 17592186325740 13194139535000 true] [17592186325736 1089 "q-336275" 13194139535000 true] [17592186325736 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535000 true] [17592186325736 1100 "The centers of two objects of masses $(6.5\\times {10}^{5})\\,\\text{kg}$ and $(4.2\\times {10}^{6})\\,\\text{kg}$ are separated by a distance of $(7.5\\times {10}^{2})\\,\\text{m}.$ According to Newton's law of universal gravitation, what is the magnitude of